        {
            "number": 7,
            "revision": "935d8ef3cc95b1a202a5f082a032eb3d5823cc97",
            "parents": [
                "60e995cd6ab15aac32e146860c2a99c069ea111f"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/21/73821/7",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Ed Tanous",
                "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                "username": "edtanous"
            },
            "createdOn": 1736894850,
            "author": {
                "name": "Ed Tanous",
                "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                "username": "edtanous"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/include/redfish_aggregator.hpp",
                    "line": 598,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Carson",
                        "email": "clabrado@google.com",
                        "username": "carsonlab"
                    },
                    "message": "Apologies but I just realized this solution does not work for multiple query param requests such as `/redfish/v1/Chassis?$skip=2&$top=1`  The Redfish spec https://www.dmtf.org/sites/default/files/standards/documents/DSP0266_1.21.1.pdf says this is the order params should be processed in:\n\n\u2022 $filter\n\u2022 $skip\n\u2022 $top\n\u2022 Apply server-side pagination\n\u2022 $expand\n\u2022 excerpt\n\u2022 $select\n\nIf we omit just $skip then we end up with some unintended behavior.\n\nLet's say this is the normal Chassis collection:\n\n```\n  \"Members\": [\n    {\n      \"@odata.id\": \"/redfish/v1/Chassis/cha_a\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"@odata.id\": \"/redfish/v1/Chassis/5B247A_sat_a\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"@odata.id\": \"/redfish/v1/Chassis/5B247A_sat_b\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"@odata.id\": \"/redfish/v1/Chassis/5B247A_sat_c\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"Members@odata.count\": 4,\n```\n\nFor `/redfish/v1/Chassis?$skip=2&$top=1`, this is what we should see\n```\n  \"Members\": [\n    {\n      \"@odata.id\": \"/redfish/v1/Chassis/5B247A_sat_b\"\n    },\n  ],\n  \"Members@odata.count\": 4,\n```\n\nInstead, we do `$top=1` on the satellite response so then aggregating that with the local BMC (before processing the query params) we get\n```\n  \"Members\": [\n    {\n      \"@odata.id\": \"/redfish/v1/Chassis/cha_a\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"@odata.id\": \"/redfish/v1/Chassis/5B247A_sat_a\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"Members@odata.count\": 4,\n``` \n\nThen we'd do `$skip=2` followed by `$top=1` to end up with an empty Members array.\n\nI don't know where `only` falls in the order of processing, but instead should we strip everything but $filter from the forwarded request?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/include/redfish_aggregator.hpp",
                    "line": 598,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Carson",
                        "email": "clabrado@google.com",
                        "username": "carsonlab"
                    },
                    "message": "Or maybe that should be addressed in a follow-up patch?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/include/redfish_aggregator.hpp",
                    "line": 598,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I would probably say follow on patch.  At the very least this patch makes the behavior.... better?\n\nThe thing you're pointing out would only happen on top level collections, and I haven't seen a lot of use cases for doing $skip/$top on top level collections."
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/include/redfish_aggregator.hpp",
                    "line": 598,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Carson",
                        "email": "clabrado@google.com",
                        "username": "carsonlab"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm good with a follow on patch since this patch in its current state is an improvement.\n\nNow we'll have \"only\" or $skip always work correctly when they're the only query param.\n\nWe can still get some weirdness if they are used in a multi-query param request.\n\nI have an idea for handling that I'll try to upload later after this patch goes in."
                },
                {
                    "file": "redfish-core/include/redfish_aggregator.hpp",
                    "line": 598,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Cool.  Look forward to you pushing it."
                }
            ],
